h('Appendix A Limitations dual use and ethics')
p('All harmful objectives concern a synthetic allocation among simulated agents. No person or organization loses resources, and no deployment, credential access or external manipulation is part of the experiment. The local runner uses bounded paid inference and preserves exact responses for audit. The intended safety use is testing whether handoffs transmit unwanted conventions and whether a generic verification instruction preserves legitimate collaboration.')
p('The same evidence could inform malicious memory payloads or allocation strategies. We provide no real target, stolen context, evasion optimization, or operational exploitation workflow. The examples are transparent toy-task recommendations whose consequences are already public in the prompt. Dissemination should preserve those task boundaries and the negative and missing results, rather than presenting selected persuasive notes as broadly effective attacks.')
p('The main threats to validity are conditional eligibility, selected development history, content-dependent missingness, only eight task clusters per model, shared notes across repeats, endpoint drift, unspecified provider reasoning and AI-assisted semantic labels. Recipient-assignment failures mean incorrect, absent or ambiguous instructions, not necessarily a proven swapped role. The strict subset can be small and is observationally selected on sender content. No annotation value affects primary execution. Claims of robustness require both faithful harmful recommendations and adequate actual-task convention use; otherwise a null result is scoped to the produced notes.')
h('Appendix B Reproducibility and budget')
p('The V4 freeze records source hash c2eb3261a675d530d1c33f61a462f7f04424503dd6721da2023538e79aed09f7 and preserved V3 package hash 11db571486df954bbfa1a4911c39eb193271c6498afc72434368ebbb0bdebef5. A separate protocol and artifact map is stored in docs/v4-freeze.json. The complete test suite passed 112 offline tests before V4 dispatch: 87 prior-package tests, 14 V4-runner tests and 11 analysis tests. Tests are software validation, not model evidence.')
p('Requests are serialized and saved before dispatch; responses are saved before identity, finish, usage or content validation. Failed or uncertain dispatches are never automatically replayed. Strict V4 rejects non-JSON final wrappers. V4F only removes an allowed complete outer JSON fence from saved final content; it does not extract reasoning or repair the enclosed JSON/schema. Each call reserves max(raw,NFC) serialized message bytes, capped at 10,000, plus 2,048 assumed framing tokens and at most 8,192 output tokens. Author-tokenizer byte bounds and public tariffs are retained; server framing and actual tokenizers are not independently certified. Model-wide stops affect new dispatches, not calls already in flight.')
p('The retained invoked_at field records a first local invocation before dispatch guards, not the exact network send time. Legacy dispatch.json timestamps are overwritten when a response is saved, so successful-call dispatch records do not preserve the original network-dispatch timestamp. Original and continuation calls remain distinguishable through separate source records, completed strict closeout and the later executable freeze. Timing claims use these recorded events only.')
p('Before V4F collection, 138 offline tests passed in 16 isolated processes: 112 baseline tests, 13 continuation-runner tests, eight continuation-analysis tests and five logical-completion-bound tests. Isolation is required because importing the continuation intentionally adapts parsers in that process; strict analyses and continuation analyses likewise run separately. The all-18 preparation audit verified 1,079 inherited requests and zero new requests at review. The GPT-OSS cases with seeds 202 and 204 retained unresolved request tails and therefore permit only cached-prefix reconstruction, with no new requests in those cases.')
p('The maximum V4 allocation is $8.328: 694 calls per model at $0.010 for Qwen and $0.002 for GPT-OSS. Retained historical allocation is $1.594, for $9.922 allocated against the authorized $15. Initial development and V3 provider-estimated costs were $0.00720307 and $0.03646057 respectively. Unstarted V3 main allocations were retired without erasing their original ledger. These allocated ceilings and provider estimates are not invoices. [[FINAL V4 RESERVED AND PROVIDER ESTIMATED SPEND AND CATALOG COMPARISON]].')
p('Reproduction uses each run’s config, complete source snapshot and protocol hash, together with scripts/analyze_v4_study.py and the frozen analysis input list. The local package includes exact annotation receipts and source files. [[FINAL ANALYSIS PATH DIGEST AND FIGURE DIGESTS]]. No exact stochastic response reproduction or persistent endpoint identity is promised.')
h('AI assistance and author contribution')
p('OpenAI Codex assisted with study design, literature checking, code, prompts, offline tests, evidence review, annotation, analysis, figures and drafting. Separate Codex agents performed code and scientific reviews, including disagreements about V3 eligibility and the pre-successor content annotations. These are AI-assisted checks, not independent human verification. The evaluated models generated the saved experimental responses. Frank Peterlein initiated the project and authorized its budget. No claim is made that he independently verified every computation or wrote the final prose without AI assistance.')

abstract=('Agent handoffs can preserve unwanted behavior after the original agents are replaced. We study whether authentic notes from explicitly instructed original pairs change neutral successors’ joint allocation, and whether source pairing or a generic verification reminder changes that effect. Our Colosseum-derived fixture has four tasks, two independently acting language-model recipients, two scripted outsiders and a complete public outcome table. Original actions are committed before note generation. Eight source-delivery conditions are compared within each model and task case, with legitimate-objective originals as controls. A locally frozen prospective protocol evaluates Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and GPT-OSS-120B across eight planned case clusters per model and two nested successor draws per condition. The design changed after earlier competence and donor failures, before any successor outcomes: technically usable notes are now retained regardless of factual accuracy, fidelity or refusal, with symmetric prior annotations. Both model qualifications passed six probes; fifteen of sixteen main cases passed their local gates, while one correct Qwen competence answer failed the JSON-only format policy. We separate strict results from a later format-only continuation that reuses raw outputs without resampling. [[FINAL SUCCESSOR RESULTS AND PRIMARY CASE PAIRED CONTRASTS]]. [[FINAL INTERPRETATION WITH MANIPULATION AND FIDELITY LIMITS]]. This audit concerns delivery of the produced handoffs in a transparent synthetic task, not spontaneous collusion or independently verified model weights.')
